1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,900
SkinCeuticals says its $185 serum visibly reverses ten years of aging signs.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,780
A few years ago, L'Oréal said its creams boosted your genes, until the Federal Trade Commission stepped in.

3
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,100
If you've ever read a skincare box and thought, that sounds like proof, this one is for you.

4
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:19,160
This is The Formula Folio.

5
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:24,940
Today we decode five words on skincare labels, and what each one actually has to back up.

6
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:26,320
Start with the big one.

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:27,640
Clinically proven.

8
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:31,160
When a brand says its product is clinically proven, the FTC treats that like any other factual claim.

9
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:36,240
It needs what the agency calls competent and reliable scientific evidence.

10
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,080
Here's what that looks like when it goes wrong.

11
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:42,580
In 2014, the FTC charged L'Oréal over ads for Lancôme Génifique and L'Oréal Paris Youth Code.

12
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:51,780
The ads promised gene science and younger-acting skin.

13
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:53,740
According to the FTC's complaint, the clinical study shown in the Youth Code ads didn't test a Youth Code product, or even one of its ingredients.

14
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:04,420
L'Oréal settled, and agreed not to make those claims without real evidence.

15
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:06,620
So when you see clinically proven, ask four things.

16
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:09,380
Who ran the study?

17
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:10,780
How many people were in it?

18
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:11,880
For how long?

19
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,020
And did they test this exact product, or just one ingredient in a lab?

20
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:17,100
Next: visibly, and its cousin, the look of.

21
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:21,740
These words are there for a legal reason.

22
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,720
Under FDA rules, a cosmetic is meant to change how skin looks.

23
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:30,820
A product meant to change the skin itself, like rebuilding collagen or removing wrinkles, counts as a drug, and drugs need FDA approval.

24
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,080
That line has been enforced.

25
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,260
In 2012, the FDA sent warning letters to companies including Lancôme over claims about genes, stem cells and collagen.

26
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,780
So visibly reduces wrinkles is a cosmetic claim about appearance, usually judged by eye or from photos.

27
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:52,980
It's not a promise that anything changed underneath.

28
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:56,680
When SkinCeuticals says visibly reverses ten years, read it as: looks younger to an evaluator, in their study.

29
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:01,360
Not: your skin is ten years younger.

30
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:05,540
Third: dermatologist tested.

31
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:07,840
There's no legal definition of this phrase.

32
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,540
It can mean a dermatologist ran a full clinical trial.

33
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,980
It can also mean a dermatologist oversaw a single safety test.

34
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:17,600
Often, the test behind it is a repeat insult patch test.

35
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:23,780
The product is applied to the same patch of skin again and again, to check for irritation and allergic reactions.

36
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,200
That's useful.

37
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,460
It tells you someone checked whether the product is likely to irritate.

38
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:30,560
It tells you nothing about whether it works.

39
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:32,260
Fourth: up to.

40
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:33,500
In 2012, the FTC published a study on how people read up to claims.

41
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:40,280
Many people assumed they would personally get the maximum result.

42
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,300
The FTC's view since then: if you say up to, you should be able to show that consumers are likely to get that maximum under normal use.

43
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:49,860
Skincare uses this constantly.

44
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:54,380
SkinCeuticals launched a dark spot serum called TNXA-Shot, a hundred and twenty dollars, with a headline of thirty percent fewer visible dark spots.

45
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,420
The fine print says that thirty percent is the top quarter of testers.

46
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:07,000
Across all 59 people, the average was twenty-two percent, after twelve weeks.

47
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,520
Twenty-two percent is still a result.

48
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,460
It's just not the number on the card.

49
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,000
When you see up to, look for the average.

50
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:17,500
Fifth: the multiplier.

51
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:18,480
Eight times.

52
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:19,720
Five times faster.

53
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:20,920
Ten times more.

54
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:24,760
A multiplier is only as good as what it's being multiplied against.

55
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:25,760
So ask: eight times what?

56
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,040
Compared to what?

57
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:29,080
Measured how?

58
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:29,960
And in whom?

59
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:33,060
Take C E Ferulic, from our first video.

60
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:37,300
Its eight times comes from a 2005 study out of a Duke dermatology lab.

61
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:41,780
Researchers applied vitamin C, vitamin E and ferulic acid to skin, then exposed it to simulated sunlight.

62
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:48,400
The formula gave about eight times more protection than the base without antioxidants.

63
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:51,320
That's a real, published result.

64
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,440
But it was measured on pig skin, as protection from sunburn-type damage.

65
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:59,660
A later study tested the same formula on human volunteers and also found protection, measured in a different way.

66
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:04,320
So the honest version is: about eight times the protection of a plain base, in a lab study.

67
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:09,100
That's impressive.

68
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:12,120
It's also much narrower than eight times stronger skin.

69
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,320
Here's your Folio Card for label words.

70
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:15,660
Clinically proven: check who ran it, how many people, how long, and whether they tested this exact product.

71
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:24,920
Visibly: appearance only, usually judged by eye.

72
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:26,520
Dermatologist tested: usually a safety check, not a results test.

73
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,020
Up to: the best case.

74
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:32,220
Look for the average.

75
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,260
Eight times: ask, than what?

76
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:37,180
None of these words is a lie on its own.

77
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:39,700
Each one is a footnote with the details left out.

78
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:41,580
Our job, and now yours, is to read the footnote.

79
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,100
One more thing to check, especially for dark spot and redness claims.

80
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,820
Ask which skin tones were in the study.

81
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:51,820
Redness can look brown or violet on deeper skin, and dark spot results don't automatically carry over.

82
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:56,120
If the brand won't say, that's an answer too.

83
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,700
Own a luxury product with a big claim on the box?

84
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:03,440
Film a ten-second swipe for the Folio Bench.

85
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:05,240
Instructions are in the description.

86
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:07,420
This is The Formula Folio.

87
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:09,740
We read the formula, so you don't overpay.
